uit to the outdoor unit. Three separate single-zone systems are three circuits. Follow the install manual either way.'), ('fdf95284-452e-5401-8c2c-43ba2011305e'::uuid, 'And the question every customer asks, can you hide the linesets without it looking like spaghetti lol... lineset covers, run them on the back of the house, and talk about it before the install, not after. Some people hate the look of a head no matter what. Those are your ducted customers.'), ('808ddaa0-459b-57b8-b0d2-af5492482b48'::uuid, 'On who actually does the tank work: mostly a separate company. On our commercial jobs it''s always a sub with its own insurance for that work. Here''s what I look for in that sub: their permits and paperwork, every time.')) v(id, body) on v.id = r.id and v.body = r.body where r.seed_batch_id = 'fm-2026-10'$q$, e, n; end if;
  execute $q$select count(*) from posts p join (values ('74e792e7-756d-59ca-b879-631f095b9182'::uuid, 'A while back I posted about being 40 and unable to pick a trade. Kash has been mentoring me since then, and he helped me line up a ride-along in each trade, so here''s the report.

Plumbing: a day on a repipe in an old Colonial in Metuchen. Loved the problem-solving, hated my knees by 2pm.
HVAC: a changeout in a split-level in Edison in August. The attic was brutal, but the diagnosis part was interesting.
Electrical: a service upgrade in Highland Park, panel swap, PSE&G had to come out to pull the meter. That was the one where I lost track of time.

So I''m leaning electrical, and maybe the low-voltage side later because of my knees. Two questions: did anyone else pick partly because of their body? And is it crazy to plan around knees at 40?'), ('2874ad19-aa79-514a-8aac-a06236f2e60b'::uuid, 'Queens kid, still in an electrical program. Local 3 is the dream but everybody knows how hard it is. My aunt lives in North Bergen and said I could stay with her after I graduate.

I know jurisdiction is a "call them and ask" thing, which I will. But for the Jersey people: if I moved to Hudson County, would I just apply to whatever local covers there like anybody else? Is it dumb to move states for an apprenticeship? My foreman on weekends says I''m overthinking it.

edit: not trying to skip the line anywhere, just want options.'), ('0f26629a-2668-55c4-9db1-9e20928d3863'::uuid, 'Sorry if this has been covered. After reading the thread on here about keeping a paper trail, I started keeping a log. Two years at my shop now, so I already feel behind.

Right now it''s a notebook: date, job, hours, what I did. Is that enough? Do I need my boss to sign something? Should I go back and reconstruct the last two years from my pay stubs, or is that pointless? I''d rather overdo it now than find out later it''s useless.

edit: I mean for a master plumber license someday, in NJ.'), ('9c5f33f5-7dad-5095-8391-cd7aa070526b'::uuid, 'About 7 months in at the shop out of Bayonne. Boss says starting next week it''s boiler cleanings and fall service every day until it gets cold, mostly old one-pipe steam in Bayonne and JC, some hot water. Ziggy told me to ask for one new thing a week so this is me asking ahead of time.

What actually happens on a fall service for an old steam boiler? What should I be watching so I''m not just the guy holding the light? brother I''ve seen maybe three steam boilers up close.'), ('620e35ba-a7a2-5220-99e1-31e930bb0d5e'::uuid, 'Update from the 40-and-undecided guy. After my first round of ride-alongs I leaned electrical, but Kash said to do a second day in whatever I was least sure about, so last Saturday I went out with an HVAC shop doing boiler and furnace startups.

The HVAC day was the one where I didn''t check my phone once. Ten houses, every one different, and the tech explained everything while he worked. My knees were fine in the basements. An attic in August would probably be another story.

For people who picked HVAC: what made you sure? And is the heat-season-then-AC-season thing as brutal as it sounds?'), ('7a7b79c2-0486-5e0a-b82b-f7fb83780cf2'::uuid, 'Different kind of question from me. We''re finally converting our own house in Piscataway from oil to gas: split-level, oil boiler with baseboard, hot water off a tankless coil in the boiler. Got three quotes and they disagree on the chimney.

Quote 1: new gas boiler and a new gas water heater, both into the chimney with a new liner.
Quote 2: high-efficiency boiler vented out the side wall in plastic, new water heater into the existing chimney, no liner.
Quote 3: high-efficiency boiler out the side wall, a separate sidewall-vented water heater, chimney not used at all.

Quote 2 is cheapest. Quote 1''s guy says Quote 2 is going to cause problems. Who''s right, and why does the chimney matter so much? Appreciate it. Kash told me to get more than one quote, so here we are. Still doing ride-alongs too, so this is half for the house and half for me.')) v(id, body) on v.id = p.id and v.body = p.body where p.seed_batch_id = 'fm-2026-10'$q$ into n; e := 6;
  if n <> e then raise exception 'check failed: % (expected %, got %)', $q$select count(*) from posts p join (values ('74e792e7-756d-59ca-b879-631f095b9182'::uuid, 'A while back I posted about being 40 and unable to pick a trade. Kash has been mentoring me since then, and he helped me line up a ride-along in each trade, so here''s the report.

